{"author":{"id":"78f9c13831d7e4cd23da41f1921a0df29d82a91276573361524e3bf648269089"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786461277,"nonce":"VIRAkR/rkEQ0WFk3cElqub2ZuHM=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOOEv6_s8AAAABOToDeg","github-url":"https://github.com/graphcore-research/gwr/issues/195#issuecomment-5255070586"},"message":"During the review of #334 the following issue was flagged:\n```\n[P2] Propagate completion through every tensor successor — [gwr-timetable/src/lib.rs (line 561)](/Volumes/scratch/repos/gwr/gwr-timetable/src/lib.rs:561)\nOnly direct tensor successors are completed. A valid control chain such as load0 → gate1 → gate2 → load1 stalls because completing gate1 never checks whether gate2 is now complete. Reproduction ended with “4 tasks completed out of 6.” Process newly completed tensors recursively or with a queue.\n```\nHowever there is currently no support for control edges.\n\nFurther explanation of the issue flagged follows:\n\nThe scheduler propagates completion through only one tensor node. A chain containing two consecutive tensor “gate” nodes therefore deadlocks.\n\nConsider this valid graph:\n\n```text\ninput0 ─data→ load0 ─control→ gate1 ─control→ gate2 ─control→ load1\ninput1 ─data───────────────────────────────────────────────→ load1\n```\n\n`gate1` and `gate2` are tensor nodes; `load0` and `load1` are executable PE tasks.\n\nInitially:\n\n- `input0` and `input1` complete because they are root tensors.\n- `load0` becomes ready.\n- `load1` remains blocked by `gate2`.\n\nWhen `load0` completes, [set_task_completed](/Volumes/scratch/repos/gwr/gwr-timetable/src/lib.rs:533) does the following:\n\n1. It recognizes direct successor `gate1`.\n2. `update_complete_tensor(gate1)` marks `gate1` complete.\n3. It calls `mark_successors_updated(gate1)`, which encounters `gate2`.\n4. Because `gate2` has no PE, [mark_dependency_completed](/Volumes/scratch/repos/gwr/gwr-timetable/src/lib.rs:257) immediately returns.\n5. Nothing calls `update_complete_tensor(gate2)`.\n\nThe propagation stops here:\n\n```text\ninput0 ✓ → load0 ✓ → gate1 ✓ → gate2 ✗ → load1 ✗\ninput1 ✓ ────────────────────────────────→\n```\n\nThe existing test passes because it has only one gate:\n\n```text\nload0 → gate → load1\n```\n\nOnce `gate` completes, its successor is directly an executable PE task, so the scheduler decrements `load1`’s dependency count. Adding another tensor exposes the missing recursion.\n\nThe runtime reproduction completed four of six nodes, then reported:\n\n```text\n4 tasks completed out of a total of 6 tasks.\n```\n\nThe fix should propagate through newly completed tensors until reaching a fixed point, typically with a queue:\n\n```text\nqueue = [completed task]\nwhile queue is not empty:\n    completed = queue.pop()\n    for successor in completed.successors:\n        if successor is a tensor:\n            if it has now completed:\n                queue.push(successor)\n        else:\n            decrement the successor task's dependency count\n```\n\nInitialization should similarly compute tensor completion to a fixed point instead of relying on a single node-order pass.","files":null}]}